  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass east of the New Jersey \nShore Sunday through Sunday night.\n\nBroad high pressure centered near the Gulf of Maine continues to\ntrack out to sea tonight. Return flow behind the departing high\nwill result in increasing low level moisture over the area late\ntonight. Clouds will increase late tonight, and begin to lower\nby daybreak. Some patchy fog may develop in the pre-dawn hours.\nA warmer night on tap with lows in the 40s.\n\nSurface low pressure continues to organize and develop off the\nFlorida coast, and this low will track north tonight towards the\nMid-Atlantic. \n\nThe 12Z/21 suite of guidance is coming in and is focusing on\nthat low taking more of an eastward track, mainly along or just\noff the New Jersey coast Sunday night. Rain moves into Delmarva\nin the morning, but the bulk of the rain really will not arrive\nuntil Sunday afternoon. The heaviest of the rain will fall along\nthe Jersey Shore in the evening. Although this will be right\naround the time of high tide, the evening high tide will be the\nlower of the two high tide cycles. Some minor flooding is\npossible near the coasts due to the combination of high tide \nand the heavy rainfall, but widespread flash flooding is not\nexpected. Rainfall amounts will mostly be 1/2 inch or less north\nand west of the I-95 corridor, and from 3/4 to 1 inch over\nDelmarva and portions of New Jersey mainly east of the Garden\nState Parkway.\n\nRain tapers off Sunday night, and conditions will dry out on\nMonday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A low pressure system will affect the weather \nfor the Middle Atlantic area on Thanksgiving day. \n\nAfter a few days of fair and cool weather Mon/Tue/Wed, conditions\nwill deteriorate across the area Wed night and last into Thursday.\nLow pressure will develop across the East Coast and head northward\nThu-Fri while strengthening. \n\nThe models are still showing some important differences with regards\nto the details of the system (not surprising at this time range).\nThe ECMWF 12Z for today is notably faster with the starting time\nof the precip across the area. The other two major models somewhat\nslower, but also showing differing surface low positions and strengths.\nWe'll use the latest WPC/NBM offerings with minor changes in our\nupdate. P-type strongly favors Rain for all areas with perhaps a\nfew flakes for southern Poconos or Lehigh Valley if precip starts\nbefore dawn Thu. A significant snowstorm is NOT expected at this\ntime. Strong/Gusty winds could be a factor if the low deepens as\na few models are indicating. Rainfall of 1 to 2 inches could occur\nfor the Thu/Fri period, again something that will be updated as the\nweek goes on. \n\nFor now, the period centered around Thanksgiving Day bears watching\nas much of the guidance agrees that a disturbace will be in the\narea. Continue to monitor future forecasts are the week progresses.",
